From: Igor Pecovnik <igor@armbian.com>
Date: Sat, 13 Sep 2026 13:20:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] k3-pico-itx: disable non-functional onboard Bluetooth

The onboard Realtek RTL8852BE Bluetooth radio (USB 0bda:b85b, on the
BT-only usb3_portc / usb3@81700000 controller) never completes HCI
initialisation on this board. It enumerates in a degraded state
(bcdUSB 1.00, bcdDevice 0.00) and HCI_OP_READ_LOCAL_VERSION times out
(-110) - i.e. it fails before btrtl ever requests firmware - so btusb
retries forever, spamming the console with an endless

    Bluetooth: hci0: command 0x1001 tx timeout
    Bluetooth: hci0: RTL: HCI_OP_READ_LOCAL_VERSION failed (-110)
    Bluetooth: hci0: Resetting usb device.

reset loop that also wastes power on this (PoE-fed) board. Neither the
DT reset pulse (default) nor removing reset-gpios stops it, confirming
the cause is below the reset line - most likely a missing BT sleep clock
or a USB2-PHY high-speed negotiation problem that needs board/PHY work.

Until that is root-caused, disable the BT-only USB2 port so the kernel
stops probing the dead radio. This matches the mainline SpacemiT/RISCstar
DT, which carries no onboard-managed Bluetooth node for this board.

Signed-off-by: Igor Pecovnik <igor@armbian.com>
---
 arch/riscv/boot/dts/spacemit/k3-pico.dtsi | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/arch/riscv/boot/dts/spacemit/k3-pico.dtsi b/arch/riscv/boot/dts/spacemit/k3-pico.dtsi
--- a/arch/riscv/boot/dts/spacemit/k3-pico.dtsi
+++ b/arch/riscv/boot/dts/spacemit/k3-pico.dtsi
@@ -481,7 +481,14 @@
 	phy-names = "usb2-phy";
 	pinctrl-names = "default";
 	pinctrl-0 = <&bt_reset_cfg>;
-	status = "okay";
+	/*
+	 * Onboard RTL8852BE BT (USB 0bda:b85b) never completes HCI init: it
+	 * enumerates as USB 1.00 / bcdDevice 0.00 and READ_LOCAL_VERSION times
+	 * out before firmware is requested, leaving btusb/btrtl in an endless
+	 * USB-reset loop. Disable the BT-only USB2 port until the root cause
+	 * (BT sleep clock / USB2-PHY high-speed negotiation) is fixed.
+	 */
+	status = "disabled";

 	bluetooth@1 {
 		/* RTL8852BE, reset must be deasserted before enumeration */
